const screeningArticles = {"table":{"tag":"THE COVER STORY / NOVEMBER 2026","title":"A seat at the table.","deck":"Belonging begins before anybody says the right thing. A November invitation to watch, listen, and make room.","image":"ladybird","credit":"Lady Bird (2017). Publicity image courtesy of A24.","body":"<p>Before a family gathering begins, the room already tells a story. Somebody is bringing an extra chair. Somebody knows which glass another person always chooses. Somebody is working out where to sit so that the evening will be easier. These small arrangements are a kind of language, even when nobody agrees on what they mean.</p><p>November makes that language more visible. A journey home, an invitation to a friend’s apartment, a meal with people who have known you for years: each asks you to occupy a place that might no longer fit in the way it once did. A seat can be waiting for you and still feel difficult to take.</p><h3>Not everybody arrives at the same home.</h3><p>This issue places three films beside that uncertainty. <em>Lady Bird</em> gives us a mother and daughter whose affection does not make understanding automatic. <em>After Yang</em> asks what a family has failed to notice about a life inside its own home. <em>Minari</em> follows the work of making a place where different hopes can coexist. These films belong to different worlds. What connects them here is a question about attention: how much of another person do we overlook because we believe we already know them?</p><p>A family film does not have to be reassuring. Sometimes its most generous gesture is to keep a disagreement in view. The person who wants to leave may also want to be missed. The person who offers practical advice may be trying, unsuccessfully, to express fear. A film can hold both positions long enough for us to hear more than the argument.</p><blockquote>A place at the table is an invitation. Understanding takes longer.</blockquote><h3>Start with what you noticed.</h3><p>At a shared screening, it is tempting to decide who was right. Try delaying that decision. Ask which gesture stayed with somebody: a glance, a hesitation, a body that turns towards the door. Two people can watch the same scene and leave with different evidence. The conversation becomes more interesting when those details have room to remain different.</p><p>The digital edition changes how SEEN travels. A printed copy passes from hand to hand; a page can cross cities in a moment. But sending a link is only the beginning. Add a person’s name. Explain why a film made you think of them. Ask a question that cannot be answered by a rating. A recommendation becomes an act of attention when it leaves room for a response.</p><p>There is no requirement to gather a perfect family around a perfect meal. Watch with a flatmate. Call somebody far away. Make a small plan with the person you keep meaning to see. The room does not have to look like home before it can begin to feel like one.</p><div class=\"conversation\"><strong>TAKE IT TO THE TABLE</strong>Choose one of this issue’s films. Before discussing the ending, each person names one small gesture they remember. Let the differences begin the conversation.</div>"},"ladybird":{"tag":"CLOSE READING / LADY BIRD","title":"The distance between us.","deck":"How a mother and daughter can share a city, a car, a whole life—and still struggle to find the same language.","image":"ladybird-mother","credit":"Lady Bird (2017). Saoirse Ronan and Laurie Metcalf. A24 publicity image.","body":"<p>In <em>Lady Bird</em>, proximity is not the same as understanding. A mother and daughter can sit beside each other and still seem to be travelling towards different destinations. Greta Gerwig gives their relationship a recognisable rhythm: closeness interrupted by irritation, a shared response overtaken by a sentence that lands badly. Love is present. It does not make the next exchange easy.</p><p>Christine’s chosen name, Lady Bird, asks other people to recognise a version of her that she is still inventing. It is a declaration before it is an explanation. To her, the name opens a future. Inside a family, a new name can also sound like a rejection of the life that came before it. The same words arrive differently depending on who is listening.</p><h3>The small correction.</h3><p>Marion often speaks in the practical language of money, work, appearance, and consequences. Lady Bird wants a response that begins somewhere else: with the possibility that her desire matters before it can be justified. Neither is simply refusing to listen. They keep recognising different problems inside the same conversation.</p><p>That mismatch makes an ordinary correction feel larger than itself. A comment about a dress can contain a fear about the future. A complaint about Sacramento can sound like a verdict on everyone who stayed there. The object being discussed becomes a place to put a feeling that is harder to name.</p><p>The film lets those feelings share space. Their arguments do not erase their capacity for tenderness, and tenderness does not settle the argument. A shared activity can briefly give them a rhythm in which neither has to defend a complete position. Then another word arrives, and the distance opens again.</p><blockquote>Leaving a place can be the beginning of learning how closely you looked at it.</blockquote><h3>A city seen from elsewhere.</h3><p>Lady Bird imagines that an elsewhere will make her life more legible. Distance promises to separate the person she wants to become from the person her family already knows. But departure does not produce a clean division. A place travels with you through the things you notice, the comparisons you make, and the people you find yourself wanting to call.</p><p>For a November screening, this is a useful complication. Coming home is often treated as a return to something stable. Gerwig’s film suggests another possibility: the home you return to is affected by the way you have learned to see it. You may notice work that once seemed automatic, or hear affection inside a sentence that previously sounded only like criticism. That does not mean the criticism stops hurting.</p><p>The most interesting conversation after this film may not be about which character deserves an apology. It may be about how affection becomes difficult to recognise when it arrives in an unwanted form. What would have to change for a practical question to sound like care? What kind of answer could acknowledge the care without surrendering a different future?</p><p>There is room for both questions. A mother and daughter do not have to reach the same destination for the journey between them to matter. The film asks us to remain attentive to that journey, especially when the people inside it cannot yet describe it to one another.</p><div class=\"conversation\"><strong>WATCH WITH SOMEBODY WHO KNEW YOU BEFORE</strong>What did leaving home help you notice about it? Start with a place or a gesture, rather than a general lesson.</div><p><small>Film details and publicity imagery: <a href=\"https://a24films.com/films/lady-bird\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A24 — Lady Bird</a>. This essay is an original interpretive draft for SEEN’s digital edition.</small></p>"},"yang":{"tag":"MEMORY / AFTER YANG","title":"The things we keep.","deck":"A life remembered in fragments. What do the smallest moments leave behind?","image":"yang","credit":"After Yang (2022). Publicity image courtesy of A24.","body":"<p>A family can become accustomed to a presence without being curious about it. In Kogonada’s <em>After Yang</em>, the failure of an artificial companion changes the attention available to the life he has been living. Jake begins with a practical problem. The attempt to repair Yang gradually becomes an encounter with what the family did not know.</p><p>The distinction matters. Repair assumes a return to function: get something working so that ordinary life can continue. Attention asks a less manageable question. What counted as ordinary to the person whose life is now being examined? A household routine can look very different from inside somebody else’s memory.</p><h3>A life without a summary.</h3><p>Fragments resist the neatness of a biography. A brief image cannot explain every relationship around it. It can only insist that a moment existed and was worth keeping. Kogonada allows that smallness to carry weight. The film’s quieter passages ask us to spend time with images without demanding that every one become evidence for a single conclusion.</p><p>This changes the position of the viewer. Instead of learning a secret that reorganises everything into a clear answer, we encounter the limits of what any person can know about another. Intimacy includes that limit. Living beside someone gives access to a shared routine; it does not grant ownership of their interior life.</p><blockquote>A memory can be small without being slight.</blockquote><p>The idea is particularly moving in a family. We often ask family photographs to prove continuity: here we were, together, on this day. But a person may remember the pause before the photograph, or a corner of the room outside its frame. The record of being together and the experience of being together do not have to coincide.</p><h3>What would you save?</h3><p>November is full of occasions that arrive already labelled as important. A gathering, a holiday, a return. <em>After Yang</em> opens space for moments that do not announce themselves in advance. A person looking towards someone else. A changing light. The ordinary interval before the next thing happens.</p><p>That attention need not make every minute precious in an exhausting way. It can be simpler: acknowledge that the part of the day you forget may have mattered to someone beside you. Ask what they noticed. Listen without correcting their version into yours.</p><p>After the film, try describing a memory without explaining why it matters. Let the physical details arrive first. A room, a colour, a sound, the position of another body. The explanation may come later. Or the details may already say enough.</p><div class=\"conversation\"><strong>A SMALL EXERCISE AFTER WATCHING</strong>Each person writes down one ordinary moment from this week they would keep. Read them aloud. Ask one question about a detail you would otherwise have missed.</div><p><small>Film information and imagery: <a href=\"https://a24films.com/films/after-yang\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A24 — After Yang</a>. Original interpretive draft for SEEN.</small></p>"},"minari":{"tag":"BELONGING / MINARI","title":"A home still taking shape.","deck":"What travels with a family when the place around them changes?","image":"minari","credit":"Minari (2021). Publicity image courtesy of A24.","body":"<p>Moving into a house does not mean that everyone has arrived at the same home. In Lee Isaac Chung’s <em>Minari</em>, a Korean American family’s move to a farm carries different promises for the people making it. A new place can look like a beginning to one person and a risk to another. The physical address is shared. The future attached to it is not.</p><p>The film’s domestic world matters because those differences must be lived through ordinary days. Work has to be done. Children need attention. Familiar routines have to be adapted. A disagreement about a large ambition enters the small arrangements through which the household continues.</p><h3>Who gets to recognise home?</h3><p>When a grandmother arrives, the question becomes more intimate. A family role comes with expectations about how somebody should behave. Recognition can fail when an actual person does not resemble the idea that preceded them. To belong to a family is not necessarily to perform the version of family another person has imagined.</p><p>That mismatch makes room for change. A relationship can develop through habits that are initially puzzling, through shared time that does not immediately become affection. Chung’s film allows care to remain practical and imperfect. People can affect one another before they fully understand the terms of the relationship.</p><blockquote>Home is also the work of making room for somebody else’s version of it.</blockquote><p>The title offers a way to think about a home that is still becoming. Minari can suggest continuity without requiring an exact reproduction of the place left behind. Something can take root elsewhere and still carry a history. The new setting changes what growing will mean; it does not erase where the plant came from.</p><h3>A place made together.</h3><p>For a viewer living between languages or cities, it can be tempting to ask which place is the real home. The film makes a different question available: what acts allow people to make a life together here? The answer may be less visible than a successful plan. It may appear in the willingness to remain attentive when a plan no longer explains what the family needs.</p><p>This does not mean that hardship automatically makes a family closer. Different hopes still have consequences. What matters is that belonging cannot be measured only against one person’s success. The room has to contain the people whose lives are affected by the dream.</p><p>A November gathering can make that question immediate. What do we ask people to leave outside when they join us? Which language is easiest to speak, and for whom? Which stories are treated as the family’s story? A shared meal might be a place to ask, provided there is room to hear an answer that changes the evening.</p><div class=\"conversation\"><strong>PASS THIS TO SOMEONE MAKING A HOME</strong>What small ritual makes an unfamiliar place feel like yours? Recommend the film with that ritual in mind.</div><p><small>Film details and imagery: <a href=\"https://a24films.com/films/minari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A24 — Minari</a>. Original interpretive draft for SEEN.</small></p>"}};
